comments[2]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-08-11T05:25:46.048Z","harness:claude-code","Measurement and implementation map, taken on the live tracker at 2026-08-11.\n\nThe asymmetry\nAn item file on disk encodes a dependency block as a length-marked tabular header naming the row count and the column list, followed by one CSV row per dependency, and encodes tags as a length-marked inline list. That is the TOON tabular form and it is what makes the storage format compact.\n\nThe renderer behind --output-format toon does not have that branch. Reading it end to end: the array case emits one dash-prefixed block per entry and recurses, and the object case emits one key-colon-value line per field. There is no uniform-shape detection, no column list, and no length marker anywhere in the output path. What the CLI calls toon is a YAML-shaped dialect that happens to share the name.\n\nMeasured cost, rows only, envelope excluded\n- activity default compact, 20 rows over 4 columns: 2046 bytes expanded, 1307 tabular, 36 percent smaller.\n- list --limit 20, 20 rows over 4 columns: 3948 expanded, 3074 tabular, 22 percent smaller.\n- next, 5 rows over 15 columns: 2062 expanded, 1405 tabular, 32 percent smaller.\n\nThe saving grows with row count and shrinks with column count, which is the opposite of how the current renderer behaves - it pays a key name per field per row regardless.\n\nWhy this is worth separating from the two command items\nThe stats and activity items each change one command. This changes the cost of every command that returns a row collection: list, list-open, list-all, list-closed, search, next, context, activity, history, deps, aggregate, duplicates, graph and the annotation readers. It is also the only one of the four that an agent can verify against a second implementation already in the repository, because the storage encoder demonstrates the target form.\n\nImplementation notes\n- Detection has to be conservative: tabular only when every entry is a flat object with an identical key set and no nested values, otherwise fall back to today's expanded form. A mixed array must not silently lose fields.\n- Escaping has to satisfy the existing toon decoder, which is the round-trip oracle already present in the tree.\n- The active encoding needs to be discoverable, otherwise a parser written against expanded output breaks silently on the first uniform collection. That discoverability lands on whatever survives the row-contract suppression work.\n"
  "2026-08-11T09:18:21.139Z","harness:codex","Evidence 2026-08-11: uniform flat object arrays now use the canonical @toon-format/toon tabular encoding; mixed and nested values retain the recursive fallback. Strict decoder round trips, escaping cases, output snapshots, packed CLI usage, and the 100% coverage run all pass. Token budgets were ratcheted to the smaller encoding."
learnings[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-08-11T09:18:21.849Z","harness:codex",TOON's token advantage depends on selecting tabular encoding only when every row has the same flat keys; preserving the recursive fallback keeps heterogeneous and nested projections lossless.
